<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-transform-tables">
<title>Transform Tables</title>

<introduction>
<remark>
<title>Prerequisites</title>
<p>Integration, differential equations, complex exponentials,
series, piecewise functions, improper integrals, linear algebra,
and basic complex-variable notation.</p>
</remark>

<remark>
<title>Learning Objectives</title>
<p>By the end of this reference section, the reader should be able to:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>recognize integral transforms as changes of representation;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>use common Laplace-transform pairs;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>recognize inverse Laplace transforms;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>apply Laplace linearity;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>use time shifting and frequency shifting;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>use derivative and integral properties of Laplace transforms;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>use convolution;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>transform ordinary differential equations;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>recognize unilateral Laplace transforms and initial conditions;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>recognize Fourier-transform conventions;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>use common Fourier-transform pairs;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>use Fourier shift, scaling, modulation, and differentiation laws;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>recognize the convolution theorem;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>understand Parseval and Plancherel relationships;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>distinguish angular-frequency and ordinary-frequency conventions;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>recognize Fourier sine and cosine transforms;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>recognize the discrete Fourier transform;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>recognize the <m>z</m>-transform and common transform pairs;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>relate the <m>z</m>-transform to difference equations;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>recognize transform methods used in PDEs, control theory,
          signals, probability, and numerical mathematics;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>avoid common convention and scaling errors.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>
</remark>
</introduction>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-integral-transform-reference">
<title>What Is an Integral Transform?</title>
<p>An integral transform converts a function <m>f</m> into another function
<m>F</m> by an expression of form</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
F(s)
=
\int
K(s,t)f(t)\,dt,
}</md>
</p>

<p>where <m>K</m> is called the transform kernel.</p>

<p>Transforms often convert difficult operations into simpler ones.</p>

<p>For example:</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\text{differentiation}
\longrightarrow
\text{multiplication},
}</md>
</p>

<p>or</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\text{convolution}
\longrightarrow
\text{multiplication}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-laplace-transform-definition-reference">
<title>Laplace Transform Definition</title>
<p>For a suitable function <m>f(t)</m> defined for <m>t\geq0</m>, the Laplace transform
is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{L}\{f(t)\}
=
F(s)
=
\int_0^\infty
e^{-st}f(t)\,dt.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-inverse-laplace-reference">
<title>Inverse Laplace Transform</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>F(s)
=
\mathcal{L}\{f(t)\},</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
f(t)
=
\mathcal{L}^{-1}\{F(s)\}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>In practical computations, inverse transforms are often obtained from tables,
partial fractions, convolution, or complex inversion methods.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-laplace-linearity-reference">
<title>Laplace Linearity</title>
<p>For constants <m>a,b</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{L}
\{
af+bg
\}
=
a\mathcal{L}\{f\}
+
b\mathcal{L}\{g\}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-laplace-constant">
<title>Laplace Transform of a Constant</title>
<p>For <m>s&gt;0</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{L}\{1\}
=
\frac1s.
}</md>
</p>

<p>More generally,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{L}\{c\}
=
\frac{c}{s}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-laplace-power-reference">
<title>Laplace Transform of Powers</title>
<p>For nonnegative integer <m>n</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{L}\{t^n\}
=
\frac{
n!
}{
s^{n+1}
}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>More generally, for suitable <m>\alpha&gt;-1</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{L}\{t^\alpha\}
=
\frac{
\Gamma(\alpha+1)
}{
s^{\alpha+1}
}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-laplace-exponential">
<title>Laplace Transform of an Exponential</title>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{L}\{e^{at}\}
=
\frac{
1
}{
s-a
}
}</md>
</p>

<p>for</p>

<p>
<md>\operatorname{Re}(s)&gt;\operatorname{Re}(a).</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-laplace-sine">
<title>Laplace Transform of Sine</title>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{L}\{\sin bt\}
=
\frac{
b
}{
s^2+b^2
}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-laplace-cosine">
<title>Laplace Transform of Cosine</title>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{L}\{\cos bt\}
=
\frac{
s
}{
s^2+b^2
}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-laplace-sinh">
<title>Laplace Transform of Hyperbolic Sine</title>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{L}\{\sinh bt\}
=
\frac{
b
}{
s^2-b^2
}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-laplace-cosh">
<title>Laplace Transform of Hyperbolic Cosine</title>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{L}\{\cosh bt\}
=
\frac{
s
}{
s^2-b^2
}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-quick-laplace-table">
<title>Quick Laplace Transform Table</title>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{L}\{1\}
=
\frac1s
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{L}\{t\}
=
\frac1{s^2}
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{L}\{t^n\}
=
\frac{n!}{s^{n+1}}
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{L}\{e^{at}\}
=
\frac1{s-a}
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{L}\{\sin bt\}
=
\frac{b}{s^2+b^2}
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{L}\{\cos bt\}
=
\frac{s}{s^2+b^2}
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{L}\{\sinh bt\}
=
\frac{b}{s^2-b^2}
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{L}\{\cosh bt\}
=
\frac{s}{s^2-b^2}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-laplace-first-derivative">
<title>First Derivative Property</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>F(s)=\mathcal{L}\{f(t)\},</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{L}\{f'(t)\}
=
sF(s)-f(0).
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-laplace-second-derivative">
<title>Second Derivative Property</title>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{L}\{f''(t)\}
=
s^2F(s)
-
sf(0)
-
f'(0).
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-laplace-higher-derivatives">
<title>Higher Laplace Derivatives</title>
<p>For positive integer <m>n</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{L}\{f^{(n)}(t)\}
=
s^nF(s)
-
s^{n-1}f(0)
-
s^{n-2}f'(0)
-\cdots
-
f^{(n-1)}(0).
}</md>
</p>

<p>This property makes the Laplace transform especially useful for initial-value
problems.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-laplace-integral-property">
<title>Integral Property</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>g(t)
=
\int_0^t
f(\tau)\,d\tau,</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{L}\{g(t)\}
=
\frac{
F(s)
}{
s
}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-laplace-multiplication-by-t">
<title>Multiplication by <m>t</m></title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>F(s)
=
\mathcal{L}\{f(t)\},</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{L}\{t f(t)\}
=
-\frac{dF}{ds}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>More generally,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{L}\{t^nf(t)\}
=
(-1)^n
\frac{
d^nF
}{
ds^n
}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-laplace-first-shift">
<title>First Shifting Theorem</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>F(s)
=
\mathcal{L}\{f(t)\},</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{L}\{e^{at}f(t)\}
=
F(s-a).
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-unit-step-reference">
<title>Unit Step Function</title>
<p>The Heaviside unit step is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
u(t-a)
=
\begin{cases}
0, &amp; t&lt;a,\\
1, &amp; t&gt;a.
\end{cases}
}</md>
</p>

<p>The value at <m>t=a</m> is convention dependent and usually irrelevant for
ordinary transform calculations.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-laplace-second-shift">
<title>Second Shifting Theorem</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>F(s)
=
\mathcal{L}\{f(t)\},</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{L}
\{
u(t-a)f(t-a)
\}
=
e^{-as}F(s).
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-dirac-delta-laplace-reference">
<title>Impulse Transform</title>
<p>For the Dirac delta in the generalized-function sense,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{L}\{\delta(t-a)\}
=
e^{-as},
\qquad
a\geq0.
}</md>
</p>

<p>In particular,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{L}\{\delta(t)\}
=
1.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-laplace-convolution-reference">
<title>Laplace Convolution</title>
<p>The convolution of <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> on <m>[0,\infty)</m> is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
(f*g)(t)
=
\int_0^t
f(\tau)
g(t-\tau)
\,d\tau.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-laplace-convolution-theorem">
<title>Laplace Convolution Theorem</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>F(s)=\mathcal{L}\{f\},
\qquad
G(s)=\mathcal{L}\{g\},</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{L}\{f*g\}
=
F(s)G(s).
}</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{L}^{-1}\{F(s)G(s)\}
=
f*g.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-laplace-periodic-function">
<title>Periodic-Function Laplace Transform</title>
<p>If <m>f</m> has period <m>T&gt;0</m>, then under suitable conditions,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{L}\{f\}
=
\frac{
\displaystyle
\int_0^T
e^{-st}f(t)\,dt
}{
1-e^{-sT}
}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-worked-laplace-ode">
<title>Worked Example: Laplace Transform of an ODE</title>
<example>
<title>Solve a Simple Initial-Value Problem</title>
<p>Solve</p>

<p>
<md>y'+y=0,
\qquad
y(0)=1.</md>
</p>

<p>Take the Laplace transform:</p>

<p>
<md>sY(s)-1+Y(s)=0.</md>
</p>

<p>Thus</p>

<p>
<md>(s+1)Y(s)=1.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>Y(s)
=
\frac{1}{s+1}.</md>
</p>

<p>Taking the inverse transform,</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
y(t)=e^{-t}.
}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-inverse-laplace-partial-fractions">
<title>Inverse Laplace Through Partial Fractions</title>
<p>Rational transforms are often inverted by decomposing them into standard
pieces.</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>\frac{1}{s(s+1)}
=
\frac1s
-
\frac1{s+1}.</md>
</p>

<p>Thus</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{L}^{-1}
\left\{
\frac{1}{s(s+1)}
\right\}
=
1-e^{-t}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-fourier-transform-convention-reference">
<title>Fourier Transform Convention</title>
<p>One common angular-frequency convention is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\widehat{f}(\omega)
=
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}
f(x)e^{-i\omega x}
\,dx.
}</md>
</p>

<p>The inverse transform is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
f(x)
=
\frac{1}{2\pi}
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}
\widehat{f}(\omega)
e^{i\omega x}
\,d\omega.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Fourier-transform conventions vary between texts.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-symmetric-fourier-convention">
<title>Symmetric Fourier Convention</title>
<p>Another common convention uses</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\widehat{f}(\omega)
=
\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}}
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}
f(x)e^{-i\omega x}
\,dx,
}</md>
</p>

<p>with inverse</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
f(x)
=
\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}}
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}
\widehat{f}(\omega)
e^{i\omega x}
\,d\omega.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Always check the convention before using a transform table.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-ordinary-frequency-fourier">
<title>Ordinary-Frequency Fourier Convention</title>
<p>Using frequency <m>\nu</m> rather than angular frequency <m>\omega</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\widehat{f}(\nu)
=
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}
f(t)e^{-2\pi i\nu t}
\,dt.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
f(t)
=
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}
\widehat{f}(\nu)
e^{2\pi i\nu t}
\,d\nu.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-fourier-linearity-reference">
<title>Fourier Linearity</title>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{F}\{af+bg\}
=
a\widehat f
+
b\widehat g.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-fourier-gaussian-reference">
<title>Fourier Transform of a Gaussian</title>
<p>Under the angular-frequency convention</p>

<p>
<md>\widehat f(\omega)
=
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}
f(x)e^{-i\omega x}
\,dx,</md>
</p>

<p>one has</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{F}\{e^{-ax^2}\}
=
\sqrt{
\frac{\pi}{a}
}
e^{-\omega^2/(4a)}
}</md>
</p>

<p>for <m>a&gt;0</m>.</p>

<p>Thus Gaussians transform into Gaussians.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-fourier-decaying-exponential">
<title>Fourier Transform of a Decaying Exponential</title>
<p>For <m>a&gt;0</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>f(x)
=
e^{-a|x|}</md>
</p>

<p>has transform</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\widehat f(\omega)
=
\frac{
2a
}{
a^2+\omega^2
}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-fourier-rectangular-pulse">
<title>Fourier Transform of a Rectangular Pulse</title>
<p>Let</p>

<p>
<md>f(x)
=
\begin{cases}
1, &amp; |x|\leq a,\\
0, &amp; |x|&gt;a.
\end{cases}</md>
</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\widehat f(\omega)
=
\frac{
2\sin(a\omega)
}{
\omega
}
}</md>
</p>

<p>with the value at <m>\omega=0</m> interpreted by continuity as <m>2a</m>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-fourier-time-shift">
<title>Fourier Time Shift</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>g(x)=f(x-a),</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\widehat g(\omega)
=
e^{-i\omega a}
\widehat f(\omega).
}</md>
</p>

<p>Translation in physical space becomes multiplication by a phase factor in
frequency space.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-fourier-frequency-shift">
<title>Fourier Frequency Shift</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>g(x)
=
e^{iax}f(x),</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\widehat g(\omega)
=
\widehat f(\omega-a).
}</md>
</p>

<p>This is also called modulation.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-fourier-scaling">
<title>Fourier Scaling</title>
<p>For nonzero real <m>a</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{F}
\{
f(ax)
\}
(\omega)
=
\frac1{|a|}
\widehat f
\left(
\frac{\omega}{a}
\right).
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-fourier-derivative-property">
<title>Fourier Derivative Property</title>
<p>Under suitable conditions,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{F}\{f'(x)\}
=
i\omega
\widehat f(\omega).
}</md>
</p>

<p>More generally,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{F}\{f^{(n)}(x)\}
=
(i\omega)^n
\widehat f(\omega).
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-fourier-multiplication-x">
<title>Multiplication by <m>x</m> in Fourier Space</title>
<p>Under suitable regularity,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{F}\{x f(x)\}
=
i
\frac{d}{d\omega}
\widehat f(\omega).
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-fourier-convolution-reference">
<title>Fourier Convolution</title>
<p>For functions on <m>\mathbb{R}</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
(f*g)(x)
=
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}
f(t)
g(x-t)
\,dt.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-fourier-convolution-theorem">
<title>Fourier Convolution Theorem</title>
<p>Under the convention used here,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{F}\{f*g\}
=
\widehat f(\omega)
\widehat g(\omega).
}</md>
</p>

<p>Also,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{F}\{fg\}
=
\frac{1}{2\pi}
(\widehat f*\widehat g).
}</md>
</p>

<p>The constants depend on the Fourier-transform convention.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-fourier-parseval-transform">
<title>Parseval Formula</title>
<p>Under suitable hypotheses,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}
f(x)
\overline{g(x)}
\,dx
=
\frac{1}{2\pi}
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}
\widehat f(\omega)
\overline{
\widehat g(\omega)
}
\,d\omega.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-plancherel-transform-reference">
<title>Plancherel Formula</title>
<p>Taking <m>g=f</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}
|f(x)|^2
\,dx
=
\frac{1}{2\pi}
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}
|\widehat f(\omega)|^2
\,d\omega.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Thus the Fourier transform preserves energy up to normalization.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-fourier-delta-reference">
<title>Fourier Transform of the Delta Distribution</title>
<p>In the generalized-function sense,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{F}\{\delta(x)\}
=
1.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Also,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{F}\{\delta(x-a)\}
=
e^{-i\omega a}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-fourier-constant-distribution">
<title>Fourier Transform of a Constant</title>
<p>In the distributional sense,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{F}\{1\}
=
2\pi\delta(\omega)
}</md>
</p>

<p>under the angular-frequency convention used here.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-fourier-complex-exponential">
<title>Fourier Transform of Complex Exponentials</title>
<p>Distributionally,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{F}
\{
e^{iax}
\}
=
2\pi
\delta(\omega-a).
}</md>
</p>

<p>This expresses a pure sinusoid as a single frequency.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-fourier-sine-cosine-delta">
<title>Sine and Cosine in Frequency Space</title>
<p>Using</p>

<p>
<md>\cos ax
=
\frac12
(e^{iax}+e^{-iax}),</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{F}\{\cos ax\}
=
\pi
[
\delta(\omega-a)
+
\delta(\omega+a)
].
}</md>
</p>

<p>Similarly,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{F}\{\sin ax\}
=
\frac{\pi}{i}
[
\delta(\omega-a)
-
\delta(\omega+a)
].
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-fourier-sine-transform-reference">
<title>Fourier Sine Transform</title>
<p>One common convention is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
F_s(\omega)
=
\sqrt{
\frac{2}{\pi}
}
\int_0^\infty
f(x)\sin(\omega x)
\,dx.
}</md>
</p>

<p>It is especially useful for odd extensions and boundary-value problems.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-fourier-cosine-transform-reference">
<title>Fourier Cosine Transform</title>
<p>One common convention is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
F_c(\omega)
=
\sqrt{
\frac{2}{\pi}
}
\int_0^\infty
f(x)\cos(\omega x)
\,dx.
}</md>
</p>

<p>It is especially useful for even extensions.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-discrete-fourier-transform-reference">
<title>Discrete Fourier Transform</title>
<p>For data</p>

<p>
<md>x_0,x_1,\ldots,x_{N-1},</md>
</p>

<p>the discrete Fourier transform is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
X_k
=
\sum_{n=0}^{N-1}
x_n
e^{-2\pi i kn/N},
\qquad
k=0,\ldots,N-1.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-inverse-dft-reference">
<title>Inverse Discrete Fourier Transform</title>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
x_n
=
\frac1N
\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}
X_k
e^{2\pi i kn/N}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-fft-reference">
<title>Fast Fourier Transform</title>
<p>The Fast Fourier Transform, or FFT, is an algorithm for computing the DFT
efficiently.</p>

<p>A direct DFT requires approximately</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
O(N^2)
}</md>
</p>

<p>arithmetic operations.</p>

<p>FFT algorithms reduce this to approximately</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
O(N\log N).
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-dft-convolution-theorem">
<title>Discrete Convolution Theorem</title>
<p>Circular convolution in the discrete domain corresponds to multiplication of
DFTs.</p>

<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>z=x*y</md>
</p>

<p>denotes circular convolution, then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
Z_k
=
X_kY_k.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-z-transform-definition-reference">
<title><m>z</m>-Transform Definition</title>
<p>For a sequence</p>

<p>
<md>x[n],</md>
</p>

<p>the bilateral <m>z</m>-transform is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
X(z)
=
\sum_{n=-\infty}^{\infty}
x[n]z^{-n}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-unilateral-z-transform">
<title>Unilateral <m>z</m>-Transform</title>
<p>For sequences indexed by <m>n\geq0</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
X(z)
=
\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}
x[n]z^{-n}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>This form is useful for initial-value difference equations.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-z-transform-unit-impulse">
<title><m>z</m>-Transform of the Unit Impulse</title>
<p>For the discrete impulse</p>

<p>
<md>\delta[n],</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{Z}\{\delta[n]\}
=
1.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-z-transform-unit-step">
<title><m>z</m>-Transform of the Unit Step</title>
<p>For</p>

<p>
<md>u[n]
=
\begin{cases}
1, &amp; n\geq0,\\
0, &amp; n&lt;0,
\end{cases}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{Z}\{u[n]\}
=
\frac{1}{1-z^{-1}}
=
\frac{z}{z-1}
}</md>
</p>

<p>with region of convergence</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
|z|&gt;1.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-z-transform-geometric">
<title><m>z</m>-Transform of a Geometric Sequence</title>
<p>For</p>

<p>
<md>x[n]
=
a^n u[n],</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
X(z)
=
\frac{1}{1-az^{-1}}
=
\frac{z}{z-a}
}</md>
</p>

<p>with region of convergence</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
|z|&gt;|a|.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-z-transform-linearity">
<title><m>z</m>-Transform Linearity</title>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{Z}\{ax[n]+by[n]\}
=
aX(z)+bY(z).
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-z-transform-time-shift">
<title><m>z</m>-Transform Time Shift</title>
<p>For the bilateral transform,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{Z}
\{
x[n-n_0]
\}
=
z^{-n_0}X(z).
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-z-transform-convolution">
<title><m>z</m>-Transform Convolution</title>
<p>For discrete convolution</p>

<p>
<md>(x*y)[n]
=
\sum_{k=-\infty}^{\infty}
x[k]y[n-k],</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{Z}\{x*y\}
=
X(z)Y(z).
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-z-transform-difference-equations">
<title>Difference Equations and the <m>z</m>-Transform</title>
<p>The <m>z</m>-transform converts shifts such as</p>

<p>
<md>x[n-1]</md>
</p>

<p>into multiplication by powers of <m>z^{-1}</m>.</p>

<p>Therefore linear difference equations can often be transformed into algebraic
equations in <m>X(z)</m>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-worked-z-transform">
<title>Worked Example: Simple <m>z</m>-Transform</title>
<example>
<title>Transform a Geometric Sequence</title>
<p>Let</p>

<p>
<md>x[n]
=
\left(
\frac12
\right)^n
u[n].</md>
</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>X(z)
=
\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}
\left(
\frac12
\right)^n
z^{-n}.</md>
</p>

<p>This is geometric with ratio</p>

<p>
<md>\frac{1}{2z}.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
X(z)
=
\frac{
1
}{
1-\frac12z^{-1}
}
=
\frac{
z
}{
z-\frac12
},
\qquad
|z|&gt;\frac12.
}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-laplace-versus-fourier-reference">
<title>Laplace Versus Fourier Transform</title>
<p>The Laplace transform uses the kernel</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
e^{-st}
}</md>
</p>

<p>with complex variable</p>

<p>
<md>s=\sigma+i\omega.</md>
</p>

<p>The Fourier transform corresponds formally to the imaginary axis</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
s=i\omega
}</md>
</p>

<p>when the relevant convergence conditions hold.</p>

<p>Thus the Laplace transform includes exponential weighting not present in the
ordinary Fourier transform.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-fourier-versus-z-transform">
<title>Fourier Versus <m>z</m>-Transform</title>
<p>The <m>z</m>-transform is the discrete-time analogue of the Laplace transform.</p>

<p>For</p>

<p>
<md>z=e^{i\omega},</md>
</p>

<p>evaluation of the <m>z</m>-transform on the unit circle is related to the
discrete-time Fourier transform, when the region of convergence includes the
unit circle.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-transforms-differential-equations">
<title>Transforms and Differential Equations</title>
<p>The Laplace transform converts</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\text{time derivatives}
}</md>
</p>

<p>into algebraic expressions in <m>s</m>.</p>

<p>The Fourier transform converts spatial derivatives into powers of
<m>i\omega</m>.</p>

<p>This allows many differential equations to become algebraic equations in
transform space.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-fourier-heat-equation-reference">
<title>Fourier Transform and the Heat Equation</title>
<p>For</p>

<p>
<md>u_t
=
\kappa u_{xx},</md>
</p>

<p>taking the Fourier transform in <m>x</m> gives</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\widehat{u}_t
=
-\kappa\omega^2
\widehat{u}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>This is an ordinary differential equation in <m>t</m>.</p>

<p>Its solution is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\widehat u(\omega,t)
=
\widehat u(\omega,0)
e^{-\kappa\omega^2t}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-fourier-wave-equation-reference">
<title>Fourier Transform and the Wave Equation</title>
<p>For</p>

<p>
<md>u_{tt}
=
c^2u_{xx},</md>
</p>

<p>the spatial Fourier transform gives</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\widehat u_{tt}
+
c^2\omega^2
\widehat u
=
0.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Each Fourier frequency behaves as a harmonic oscillator.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-laplace-transfer-function-reference">
<title>Laplace Transform and Transfer Functions</title>
<p>For a linear time-invariant system with zero initial conditions,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
H(s)
=
\frac{
Y(s)
}{
U(s)
}
}</md>
</p>

<p>is the transfer function.</p>

<p>Here <m>U(s)</m> is the input transform and <m>Y(s)</m> is the output transform.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-impulse-response-transfer-function">
<title>Impulse Response and Transfer Function</title>
<p>If <m>h(t)</m> is the impulse response, then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
H(s)
=
\mathcal{L}\{h(t)\}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>The output is convolution:</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
y(t)
=
(h*u)(t).
}</md>
</p>

<p>In transform space,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
Y(s)
=
H(s)U(s).
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-transforms-probability-reference">
<title>Transforms in Probability</title>
<p>For random variable <m>X</m>, the characteristic function is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\varphi_X(t)
=
\mathbb{E}
[
e^{itX}
].
}</md>
</p>

<p>It is a Fourier-type transform of the probability distribution.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-moment-generating-transform-reference">
<title>Moment-Generating Function</title>
<p>The moment-generating function is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
M_X(t)
=
\mathbb{E}
[
e^{tX}
].
}</md>
</p>

<p>When it exists near <m>t=0</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
M_X^{(n)}(0)
=
\mathbb{E}[X^n].
}</md>
</p>

<p>It is closely related to a bilateral Laplace transform.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-normal-characteristic-function">
<title>Characteristic Function of a Normal Variable</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>X\sim N(\mu,\sigma^2),</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\varphi_X(t)
=
\exp
\left(
i\mu t
-
\frac12\sigma^2t^2
\right).
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-quick-laplace-properties">
<title>Quick Laplace Property Table</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>F(s)=\mathcal{L}\{f(t)\},</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{L}\{f'(t)\}
=
sF(s)-f(0)
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{L}\{e^{at}f(t)\}
=
F(s-a)
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{L}\{t f(t)\}
=
-F'(s)
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{L}\{f*g\}
=
F(s)G(s)
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathcal{L}
\{
u(t-a)f(t-a)
\}
=
e^{-as}F(s).
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-quick-fourier-properties">
<title>Quick Fourier Property Table</title>
<p>Under</p>

<p>
<md>\widehat f(\omega)
=
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}
f(x)e^{-i\omega x}\,dx,</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
f(x-a)
\longleftrightarrow
e^{-i\omega a}\widehat f(\omega)
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
e^{iax}f(x)
\longleftrightarrow
\widehat f(\omega-a)
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
f(ax)
\longleftrightarrow
\frac1{|a|}
\widehat f\left(\frac{\omega}{a}\right)
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
f'(x)
\longleftrightarrow
i\omega\widehat f(\omega)
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
xf(x)
\longleftrightarrow
i\widehat f'(\omega)
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
f*g
\longleftrightarrow
\widehat f\,\widehat g.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-transform-tables-common-errors">
<title>Common Errors</title>
<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-mixing-fourier-conventions">
<title>Mixing Fourier Conventions</title>
<p>Different texts place <m>2\pi</m> factors in different locations.</p>

<p>Never combine formulas from different conventions without adjusting their
normalization.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-confusing-frequency-with-angular-frequency">
<title>Confusing Frequency With Angular Frequency</title>
<p>Ordinary frequency <m>\nu</m> and angular frequency <m>\omega</m> satisfy</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\omega
=
2\pi\nu.
}</md>
</p>

<p>This changes the exponential kernel and transform scaling.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-forgetting-initial-conditions-in-laplace-derivatives">
<title>Forgetting Initial Conditions in Laplace Derivatives</title>
<p>The transform</p>

<p>
<md>\mathcal{L}\{f'\}</md>
</p>

<p>is not simply <m>sF(s)</m>.</p>

<p>It is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
sF(s)-f(0).
}</md>
</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-incorrect-transform-of-a-shift">
<title>Incorrect Transform of a Shift</title>
<p>A time shift and a frequency shift produce different effects.</p>

<p>For Fourier transforms,</p>

<p>
<md>f(x-a)</md>
</p>

<p>produces a phase factor, while</p>

<p>
<md>e^{iax}f(x)</md>
</p>

<p>shifts the frequency-domain function.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-ignoring-the-region-of-convergence">
<title>Ignoring the Region of Convergence</title>
<p>For Laplace and <m>z</m>-transforms, the algebraic expression alone may not
uniquely identify the original function or sequence.</p>

<p>The region of convergence is part of the transform description.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-forgetting-the-absolute-value-in-fourier-scaling">
<title>Forgetting the Absolute Value in Fourier Scaling</title>
<p>For real nonzero <m>a</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
f(ax)
\longleftrightarrow
\frac1{|a|}
\widehat f(\omega/a).
}</md>
</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-using-the-convolution-theorem-with-the-wrong-normalization">
<title>Using the Convolution Theorem With the Wrong Normalization</title>
<p>Depending on the Fourier convention, factors such as</p>

<p>
<md>2\pi</md>
</p>

<p>may appear in the convolution or product theorem.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="id-15-reference-12-transform-tables-subsubsec-treating-the-dirac-delta-as-an-ordinary-function">
<title>Treating the Dirac Delta as an Ordinary Function</title>
<p>The Dirac delta is a distribution or generalized function.</p>

<p>Formulas involving</p>

<p>
<md>\delta(x)</md>
</p>

<p>should be interpreted in the appropriate distributional sense.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-confusing-fourier-series-and-fourier-transforms">
<title>Confusing Fourier Series and Fourier Transforms</title>
<p>Fourier series describe periodic functions using discrete frequencies.</p>

<p>Fourier transforms typically describe nonperiodic functions using a
continuous frequency variable.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-confusing-the-dft-with-the-continuous-fourier-transform">
<title>Confusing the DFT With the Continuous Fourier Transform</title>
<p>The DFT operates on a finite sequence and yields a finite collection of
frequency coefficients.</p>

<p>It introduces periodicity and sampling effects not present in the continuous
transform.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-ignoring-aliasing">
<title>Ignoring Aliasing</title>
<p>When continuous signals are sampled, frequencies above the Nyquist limit can
be represented incorrectly as lower frequencies.</p>

<p>Sampling theory must be considered in numerical Fourier analysis.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-assuming-every-function-has-an-ordinary-fourier-transform">
<title>Assuming Every Function Has an Ordinary Fourier Transform</title>
<p>Some important transforms exist only in:</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
L^2
}</md>
</p>

<p>or distributional senses.</p>

<p>For example, constants and pure sinusoids naturally involve delta
distributions.</p>
</subsubsection>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-transform-method-workflow">
<title>Transform Method Workflow</title>
<p>A practical transform workflow is:</p>

<p>
<ol>
<li>
<p>identify the problem domain: continuous time, continuous space,
          discrete time, or periodic;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>choose Laplace, Fourier, Fourier series, DFT, or <m>z</m>-transform;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>state the transform convention;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>check convergence or regularity assumptions;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>apply linearity;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>use shifting, scaling, or derivative properties;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>simplify algebraically in transform space;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>use partial fractions when appropriate;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>apply an inverse transform;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>restore initial or boundary conditions;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>verify the solution in the original domain.</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-12-transform-tables-subsec-transform-tables-exercises">
<title>Exercises</title>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find</p>

<p>
<md>\mathcal{L}\{1\}.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find</p>

<p>
<md>\mathcal{L}\{t^3\}.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find</p>

<p>
<md>\mathcal{L}\{e^{2t}\}.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find</p>

<p>
<md>\mathcal{L}\{\sin3t\}.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find</p>

<p>
<md>\mathcal{L}\{\cos4t\}.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find</p>

<p>
<md>\mathcal{L}^{-1}
\left\{
\frac1{s^3}
\right\}.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
